WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:03.201
Hey, I'm Nick from the
container tools team at Google.

00:00:03.201 --> 00:00:04.950
And today, I want to
talk to you about one

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.620
of our newest and most
exciting tools, Skaffold.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.060
Skaffold is a tool
that's designed

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.296
to make local Kubernetes
development easy.

00:00:11.296 --> 00:00:13.170
It watches your local
application directories

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.730
for changes, and
can automatically

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.830
build, push, and deploy
your application images

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.080
to any local or remote
Kubernetes cluster,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.034
letting you focus on
developing your application.

00:00:22.034 --> 00:00:23.700
Let's take a look at
how to get Skaffold

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.880
running on your application.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.139
Skaffold runs as a binary
on your local machine.

00:00:29.139 --> 00:00:31.680
So installing it is as easy as
downloading the latest release

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.810
from the Google Container
Tools GitHub page.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.260
Alternatively, you can build
Skaffold straight from source.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.570
Skaffold uses YAML
configuration to describe

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.880
how your application should
be built and deployed.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.670
You can specify one or more
images for Skaffold to build,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.160
which Skaffold will tag, and
push to any valid document

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.210
repository.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.770
These continuously-built
images will

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.891
be injected into your
Kubernetes ideas manifest,

00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:54.390
and used to deploy new changes
to your cluster on every code

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.755
change.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.380
Let's walk through an example to
demonstrate a typical Skaffold

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.670
workflow.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.830
Here we have a simple go
Hello World! application,

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.110
which just prints "Hello
World!" in a loop forever.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.526
We have a docker file that
builds a binary from the source

00:01:06.526 --> 00:01:08.067
file, and puts it
into a container,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.900
and a Kubernetes manifest
that creates a pod

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.300
from this container image.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.112
Skaffold has a built-in
command, skaffold init,

00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:16.320
that will generate a Skaffold
YAML for your code base

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.770
containing default values
to get Skaffold running.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:20.970
Let's run it on the
code that we have here.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.354
You can see our generated
Skaffold YAML has one build

00:01:23.354 --> 00:01:25.770
artifact pointing to our Docker
file that builds the image

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.900
and pushes it to our
project's registry.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.600
This image is tagged with the
git tagging strategy, which

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.700
uses the latest commit
hash as an image tag,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.474
though there are several other
tagging strategies available.

00:01:35.474 --> 00:01:38.432
Skaffold also defaults to
using the Kube control deployer

00:01:38.432 --> 00:01:40.140
that deploys from our
Kubernetes manifest

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.700
to our existing
cluster, though there

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.480
are several other deployment
strategies available as well.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.030
As we run skaffold dev, you
can see Skaffold immediately

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.070
builds the artifact we
specified, and pushes it

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.900
to the target registry.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.790
It then creates a
pod in our cluster

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.250
using the image we just pushed.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.201
By default, Skaffold streams the
logs from the pods it creates,

00:01:59.201 --> 00:02:01.200
so looking at these now,
we can see Hello World!

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.800
being logged like we expect.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.740
One of Skaffold's
most exciting features

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.006
is its file watcher,
which watches for changes

00:02:08.006 --> 00:02:09.880
to one or more local
directories on your file

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.820
system in real time.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.177
When Skaffold sees any
changes to your code,

00:02:14.177 --> 00:02:15.760
it will build a new
image from source,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.430
and deploy it to your
Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.930
Let's change our
print statement up,

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.830
and watch how Skaffold reacts.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.980
As I change the string
to Hello Skaffold!

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.900
We can immediately see
Skaffold recognizes

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.000
that our source file has
changed and re-triggers

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.510
a build and deploy.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.160
Watching the logs, we can see
them switch over in real time

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.180
to our new print statement
as the new pod is created.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.070
Let's take a look at
a more complex example

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.670
to see how Skaffold works
with more realistic workflows.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.250
Here, we have an application
with two services-- a web

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.290
server and an
application server.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.570
These services can be built
and deployed independently

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.361
of each other, and
Skaffold is smart enough

00:02:51.361 --> 00:02:52.562
to understand that.

00:02:52.562 --> 00:02:55.020
Let's go through the same flow
we did with the Hello World!

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.080
Example here to see
what it looks like.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.891
Running skaffold
init again, this time

00:02:59.891 --> 00:03:01.390
we're prompted for
which Docker file

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.556
builds each image, since
there's no way for Skaffold

00:03:03.556 --> 00:03:04.690
to know that beforehand.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.820
In our generated
Skaffold YAML, we

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.370
can see that a build artifact
is specified for each services'

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:10.980
source code directory,
and Skaffold

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.230
will put a separate file watcher
on each of these directories.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.840
This means that when files
change in one directory but not

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.450
the other, only the container
corresponding to the modified

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.440
directory will be rebuilt.
skaffold dev builds and deploys

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.314
both parts of the application
as we expect, and in the logs,

00:03:26.314 --> 00:03:28.480
we can see both services
are ready in our Kubernetes

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.130
cluster.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.094
Now if I go make a
change in the web server,

00:03:32.094 --> 00:03:33.760
we can see Skaffold
picks up the change,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.740
and only rebuilds
that container.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.450
Skaffold's deployer
then only updates

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.030
the deployment associated
with the newly-built artifact,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.976
and we can see this
reflected in the deploy logs.

00:03:42.976 --> 00:03:44.592
This means your dev
cycles are never

00:03:44.592 --> 00:03:46.300
bottlenecked by parts
of your application

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.900
that you're not working on.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.450
These services don't
necessarily need

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.920
to live in the same repository.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.344
As long as your Skaffold YAML
points to valid workspaces

00:03:53.344 --> 00:03:54.760
on your local file
system, you can

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.426
use any number of
different repositories

00:03:56.426 --> 00:03:57.970
in a Skaffold
workflow, making it

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.400
an awesome tool for development
on microservice-based

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.400
applications.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.360
Skaffold can help speed up any
Kubernetes development workflow

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.120
by getting ops out of your way,
allowing you to focus solely

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.890
on application development.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.140
You can find these
examples and more

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.700
in the Skaffold
GitHub repository,

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.740
linked in the description
of this video.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.652
Contributions to Skaffold
are always welcome.

00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:18.610
If you'd like to learn
more about contributing,

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.151
check out the
contributing guidelines

00:04:20.151 --> 00:04:21.222
in our GitHub repository.

00:04:21.222 --> 00:04:23.680
Go try it out for yourself,
and let us know what you think.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:24.820
Happy Skaffolding.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

